er"},{"text":" : ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Comparable","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:SL"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"anchor":"discussion","type":"heading","text":"Discussion","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Each round makes the best improving move, the first move (i, j) in row-major order on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"ties, until no exchange of two tour edges for two lighter ones remains. The first vertex of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"tour","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" stays first. Only "},{"code":"tour.vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is read: the result uses the lightest edge of each"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pair, as every tour does. nil below three vertices."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A move’s gain is formed as (d(a, b) + d(c, e)) − (d(a, c) + d(b, e)), and the scan stops"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"when no move gains anything. With floating-point weights, rounding decides which moves"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"gain."}]},{"type":"aside","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The graph is complete; "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tour.vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" lists every vertex of the graph"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"exactly once; no weight is NaN or infinite; the sum of any two weights fits in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"W"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"name":"Precondition","style":"note"},{"type":"aside","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(n²) per improving move."}]}],"name":"Complexity","style":"note"}],"kind":"content"}],"sections":[],"references":{"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/Graph/Edges-swift.associatedtype":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Grap